rofit metrics.\n3. FY2025 OCF of $842M vs NI of $577M (attributable) \u2014 the $265M gap is mainly changes in non-controlling interests and reserves; I do not use OCF directly for an insurer's distributable earnings.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT [Tier 2 web sources, cited inline]\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 1-1. Macro Variable Check**\n| Variable | Reading | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| 10Y UST | 4.69\u20134.74% (Aug 2026), +44bp YoY | businessquant.com / macroradar.io / tradingeconomics.com [Actual] |\n| Real rates | ~2.5\u20132.8% (4.7% nominal minus ~2% CPI) | derived [Estimate] |\n| Fed stance | ON HOLD, 3.50\u20133.75%, 5th consecutive meeting; ~1/3 odds of HIKE priced | tradingeconomics.com [Actual] |\n| Q2 2026 GDP | 1.5% (vs 2.1% Q1 \u2014 sharp slowdown) | myfw.com August 2026 commentary [Actual] |\n| CPI | +0.1% m/m in July 2026, ~+2.0\u20132.2% y/y | BLS via investing.com [Actual] |\n| Credit spreads | Not retrieved; labelled [Unconfirmed] | \u2014 |\n| Global liquidity | Easing has stalled; Fed on hold, QT tail-end; neutral-to-tightening | myfw.com [Estimate] |\n| Cycle position | Late expansion, growth decelerating | [Estimate] |\n\n**Step 1-2. Regime Distribution**\n| Regime | Probability | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reflation (high rates + growth) | 40% | Rates elevated, jobs/CPI sticky; Fed on hold |\n| Stagflation (high rates + low growth) | 20% | GDP 1.5%, inflation ~2% \u2014 slowflation tail |\n| Recession (low rates + low growth) | 20% | Late-cycle risk; 1/3 odds of hike |\n| Goldilocks (low rates + growth) | 20% | Soft-landing scenario |\n| **Total** | **100%** | |\n\n**Step 1-3. Macro Sensitivity (-3 to +3)**\n| Axis | Score | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interest rates | +2.0 | $7B+ investable assets; NII $141M/qtr \u2192 $560M annualized; ~50% of earnings. Higher rates lift ROE near-term, but with a lag on reinvestment risk |\n| Dollar (DXY) | -1.0 | Stronger USD hurts non-US underwriting competitiveness |\n| Liquidity | -0.5 | Stalled easing \u2192 no credit tailwind |\n| Economic cycle | -1.0 | GDP 1.5% \u2192 lower exposure growth, premium volumes |\n| Commodities | -0.5 | Social inflation / casualty severity persists |\n\n**Step 1-4. Macro Alignment: NEUTRAL-TO-MILD HEADWIND**\nRegime-weighted macro impact \u2248 +0.2 (rate tailwind offset by growth/liquidity headwinds). Conviction -0.5 steps. Entry discipline tightened.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 1.5-1. Base Rates**\n- Cyclical (property-casualty) insurers: mid-cycle ROE reverts to ~WACC; peak ROEs (20%+) historically unsustainable beyond 2\u20133 years. Base rate: ROE > 15% sustained for >5 years \u2248 15\u201325% [Estimate].\n- 5-year survival of an established Bermudian reinsurer with capital > $3B: \u2248 95%+ [Estimate].\n- Softening market \u2192 CY combined ratios +3\u20137 pts from trough within 18 months (2017\u20132019 analogue: cat ROE fell from mid-teens to single digits) [Estimate].\n\n**Step 1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rate \u2014 Required Justifications**\nMy base case assumes ROE reverts from 22% (2025) to ~13\u201314% (2027E), which is consistent with (not above) the base rate. I do not assume an above-base-rate outcome. To justify the bull case (ROE > 16% sustained), three company-specific arguments:\n1. Two Sigma-led investment alpha has added 200\u2013400bp ROE, partially non-cyclical [Estimate].\n2. Specialty/casualty mix (61% of GWP in Q2 2026) has pricing power while property-cat (the most commoditized line) is de-emphasized \u2014 management explicitly cutting property where price < return thresholds (Q2 call, verbatim) [Actual].\n3. Low-cost Bermuda/Lloyd's platform gives structural expense advantage vs US peers [Estimate].\n\n**Step 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check**\nBull-case probability of 20% for sustained >16% ROE is below the 1.5\u00d7 threshold (\u2248 30%); no red flag. Base case stays disciplined.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u25