\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 1.5-1. Base Rates**\n- Cyclical (property-casualty) insurers: mid-cycle ROE reverts to ~WACC; peak ROEs (20%+) historically unsustainable beyond 2\u20133 years. Base rate: ROE > 15% sustained for >5 years \u2248 15\u201325% [Estimate].\n- 5-year survival of an established Bermudian reinsurer with capital > $3B: \u2248 95%+ [Estimate].\n- Softening market \u2192 CY combined ratios +3\u20137 pts from trough within 18 months (2017\u20132019 analogue: cat ROE fell from mid-teens to single digits) [Estimate].\n\n**Step 1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rate \u2014 Required Justifications**\nMy base case assumes ROE reverts from 22% (2025) to ~13\u201314% (2027E), which is consistent with (not above) the base rate. I do not assume an above-base-rate outcome. To justify the bull case (ROE > 16% sustained), three company-specific arguments:\n1. Two Sigma-led investment alpha has added 200\u2013400bp ROE, partially non-cyclical [Estimate].\n2. Specialty/casualty mix (61% of GWP in Q2 2026) has pricing power while property-cat (the most commoditized line) is de-emphasized \u2014 management explicitly cutting property where price < return thresholds (Q2 call, verbatim) [Actual].\n3. Low-cost Bermuda/Lloyd's platform gives structural expense advantage vs US peers [Estimate].\n\n**Step 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check**\nBull-case probability of 20% for sustained >16% ROE is below the 1.5\u00d7 threshold (\u2248 30%); no red flag. Base case stays disciplined.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 2-1. Business Understanding**\nHamilton is a Bermuda-based specialty insurer/reinsurer underwriting complex property, casualty, and specialty risks through three platforms: Bermuda (reinsurance), Lloyd's (international specialty insurance), and the US. It differentiates through data-driven underwriting and the Two Sigma-managed investment portfolio. **Understandability: High** \u2014 the business model is transparent even if reserving adds opacity.\n\n**Step 2-2. Moat Assessment**\n| Moat Type | Score (0\u20133) | Evidence / Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (brand, licenses) | 1 | Lloyd's license valuable; brand modest |\n| Switching costs | 1 | Brokers multi-panel; low switching costs |\n| Network effects | 0 | None |\n| Cost advantage | 2 | Low-cost Bermuda/Lloyd's structure |\n| Economies of scale | 1 | AUM $9.6B vs $50B+ for RNR/Arch |\n| Regulatory barriers | 2 | Bermuda SBF and Lloyd's licenses restrict entry |\n| Data moat | 2 | Two Sigma analytical edge; real but expiring (competitors buy same model vendors) |\n| **Total** | **9/21 \u2014 Narrow moat, NARROWING** | Evidence: property-cat reinsurance capacity abundant, rates -16% [Actual]; research brief notes HG \"lacks strong competitive moat\" vs Kinsale, Arch, RNR, SiriusPoint [Estimate\u2014csimarket/marketbeat summaries]. |\n\n**Step 2-3. Lynch Classification**\n**Cyclical** \u2014 with high staid variability in underwriting cycles and investment income. Core checkpoint: underwrite through the cycle; currently performing at peak, so quality must be judged on mid-cycle economics, not TTM results.\n\n**Step 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard**\n| Metric | FY2024 | FY2025 | Judgment |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue growth | +48% | +25% | Excellent |\n| Net income (attributable) | $400M | $577M | Excellent |\n| ROE (avg) | ~18% | ~22% | Excellent (confirmed by company release) |\n| Combined ratio | ~95% (derived) | ~91% (derived) | Good-to-excellent |\n| Investment income | \u2014 | ~$500M (derived) | Excellent |\n| Net debt / equity | Net cash (~$1.0B cash vs $0.15B LT debt; no ST debt carried) | \u2014 | Strong |\n| OCF vs reported NI | OCF $759M vs NI $613M consolidated | OCF $842M vs NI $577M attributable | Adequate \u2014 OCF includes NCI share and reserve fluctuations |\n\n**Overall: Good to Excellent.**\n\n**Step 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard**\n| Criterion | Score (0\u20133) | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency | 2 | $93.4M in 2025 at avg ~8\u201310x earnings; $42M in H1 2026; $205.8M special dividend. But SBC offsets ~30% of buybacks |\n| M&A track record | 3 | No dilutive M&A since IPO; disciplined |\n| Capex efficiency | 3 | Capital-light (minimal capex); capital channeled to underwriting |\n| Mix rationality | 2 | Buyback + special dividend, but not all below book |\n| Guidance accuracy | 2 | 2026 low-double-digit growth guidance consistent with H1 (+17% GWP); combined guided low-to-mid 90s, tracking 95% in Q2 |\n| **Total** | **12/15** | |\n| Insider bonus | +1 | Insider owns 5.07% [Tier 2]; CEO term extended through 2029 [Web, Actual] |\n| Insider selling | 0 | No cluster selling detected |\n\n**Score: 13/17 \u2014 Above average.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 2.5. ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 2.5-1/3.** No material R&D, no operating leases beyond immaterial $7M; not capitalized.\n\n**Step 2.5-2.** SBC: $29M FY2025 (~5% of attributable NI). Adjusted distributable earnings = $577M \u2212 $29M \u2248 $548M. Treating SBC as cash expense, adjusted ROE \u2248 20.9% (2025).\n\n**Step 2.5-4.** Brand/data assets: not separately valued \u2014 embedded in underwriting ROE.\n\n**Step 2.5-5.** Adjusted ROIC \u2248 adjusted ROE \u2248 20.9% (2025) vs GAAP ROE 22%; gap < 2pp \u2014 immaterial.\n\n**Step 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (2025)**\n$577M attributable NI + $16M D&A \u2212 ~$10M maintenance capex (est.) \u2212 \u0394WC (~$60M, est.) \u2248 **$523M** [Estimate]. For a P&C insurer, underwriting cash plus investment income IS the owner earnings; this is the figure the P/B model capitalizes.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3. VALUATION (ENGINE 2 + ENGINE 1 BLEND)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 0-4 Grounding.** Engine 2 selected because HG is cycle-dominant: the 2023\u20132025 hard-market tailwind (double-digit rate increases) is reversing in 2026 (property-cat rate index -16%). A straight DCF on 2025 peak earnings would overvalue by construction. Engine 1 (residual income) is retained as a cross-check, not a replacement.\n\n**Discount Rate Derivation**\n- Risk-free: 4.7% (10Y UST, Aug 2026) [Actual\u2014businessquant/macroradar]\n- ERP: 5.5% [Assumption]\n- Beta: 0.70 [Estimate \u2014 vendor's 0.437 is undisclosed-window and too low for a reinsurer's earnings cyclicality; 0.7 is the sector norm]\n- Cost of Equity = 4.7% + 0.70 \u00d7 5.5% = **8.6%; rounded to 9.0%** [Assumption]\n\n**Forecast Drivers (all years beyond 2026 are explicitly [Assumption])**\n| Year | GWP growth | Calendar-year combined ratio | Investment income | ROE (on avg BV) | BVPS (dividend-adjusted) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026E | +12% (management guidance: \"low double-digit\", Q2 call [Actual]) | 94.0% (guidance: \"low-to-mid-90s\" [Actual]) | $560M (Q2 run-rate $141M annualized [Actual]) | ~15\u201316% | $30.91 (mid-2026 actual) |\n| 2027E | +7% [Assumption \u2014 property-cat softening partially offset by casualty] | 95.5% [Assumption] | $570M [Assumption] | ~13.5% | ~$33.5 |\n| 2028E | +5% [Assumption] | 97.0% [Assumption \u2014 softening deepens] | $575M [Assumption] | ~11.5% | ~$35.5 |\n| 2029E | +4% [Assumption] | 98.0% [Assumption \u2014 trough] | $575M [Assumption] | ~10.5% | ~$36.5 |\n| 2030E | +5% [Assumption \u2014 cycle turns] | 96.5% [Assumption] | $580M [Assumption] | ~11.5% | ~$38.0 |\n| Terminal | +4.0% [Assumption, capped at nominal GDP] | 96% [Assumption] | \u2014 | 11.5% ROE, 9% CoE \u2192 P/B 1.10\u00d7 [Assumption] | \u2014 |\n\n**Engine 2 \u2014 Mid-cycle P/B \u00d7 Normalized ROE**\n- Book value per share (dividend-adjusted, Tpf): $30.91 [Actual]\n- Sustainably normal ROE: 12.5% (mid-point of my 10.5\u201315.5% cycle) [Assumption]\n- Fair P/B = (ROE \u2212 g) / (CoE \u2212 g) = (12.5% \u2212 4%) / (9% \u2212 4%) = 1.70\u00d7 \u2014 reject as too generous for a narrow-moat cyclical. Cap at observed peer band 1.0\u20131.3\u00d7 [Assumption].\n- **Base fair P/B = 1.15\u00d7 \u2192 $35.50** [Estimate]\n\n**Engine 1 \u2014 Residual Income Cross-check (from BVPS $30.91, CoE 9%, terminal ROE 12%, terminal P/B 1.10\u00d7)**\n| Year | ROE | BVPS start | Residual earnings | Discount factor | PV of residual |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2027E | 13.5% | $30.91 | 1.39 | 0.917 | 1.28 |\n| 2028E | 11.5% | $33.30 | 0.83 | 0.842 | 0.70 |\n| 2029E | 10.5% | $35.00 | 0.53 | 0.772 | 0.41 |\n| 2030E | 11.5% | $36.30 | 0.91 | 0.708 | 0.64 |\n| Terminal (2031+) | 11.5% | $38.20 | PV of terminal residual = 0.40 | 0.708 | 0.28 |\n| **Total intrinsic** | | | | | **$34.20** |\n\n**Blended Intrinsic Value = 0.70 \u00d7 $35.50 + 0.30 \u00d7 $34.20 = $35.10 \u2192 $35.50 (rounded)** [Estimate]\n\n**Tornado (top-3 drivers \u00b11\u03c3)**\n1. **Terminal/normal ROE (10% vs 13%)** \u2192 IV $31.5\u2013$40.5\n2. **Cost of equity (10% vs 8%)** \u2192 IV $32.5\u2013$39.5\n3. **2027 combined ratio (96% vs 94%)** \u2192 IV $34.0\u2013$37.0\nThese become the thesis KPIs.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n1. **Competitive response**: If HG holds premium rates while peers cut, it loses share (already visible: Bermuda GPW +5% vs International +20% in Q1 2026 \u2014 property cat shrinking). If it follows the market down, ROE compresses. Both paths converge to lower ROE. Score: -2.\n2. **Reflexivity**: Positive \u2014 high ROE + buybacks attract capital and talent, reinforcing underwriting discipline. Negative \u2014 a reserve shock (e.g., Baltimore bridge-style latent loss) would compress book, trigger scrutiny, and raise cost of capital. Current sentiment positive, so reflexivity mildly positive.\n3. **Customer behavior**: Brokers are rate-shopping in 2026; policyholders retain more risk as cat rates fall. LTV per policy declines; retention risk rises.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n| Scenario | Probability | Path | Implied BVPS 2030 | Exit P/B | Price |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Bull**: Discipline holds; Two Sigma alpha continues; soft cycle shallow | 20% | ROE 15.5% avg; combined 93% | $44 | 1.30\u00d7 | **$45.50** |\n| **Base**: Normal softening; 2027\u201329 trough ROE ~10\u201311%; recovery 2030 | 50% | Combined 95\u201398%; investment income steady | $39 | 1.15\u00d7 | **$35.50** |\n| **Bear**: Rate war + casualty reserve cycle; ROE 8% | 30% | Combined 100%+; reserve charges 2027\u201328 | $30 | 0.95\u00d7 | **$28.50** |\n| **Expected Value** | 100% | Weighted | \u2014 | \u2014 | **$35.45** |\n\n- 50% CI: $31\u2013$40 [Estimate]; 80% CI: $28\u2013$45 [Estimate]\n- Margin of Safety vs Base: **1.5% \u2014 None**\n- Bayesian update logic: prior = base rates (softening cycle); likelihood from Q2 2026 evidence (combined 95%, +17% growth, $50M cat loss absorbed) \u2192 posterior probabilities as above. If Q3/Q4 2026 combined ratios print < 93%, shift 10 pts from bear to base.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4.5. HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n- Market implied horizon: ~1.2\u00d7 book = ~10\u201311% forward ROE, i.e., the market is assuming a 2027\u201328 trough ROE **two years ahead**. The consensus 2027 EPS decline (-6.2% per vendor) corroborates that the market is NOT extrapolating 2025 peak earnings.\n- My horizon: 3\u20135 years with mean reversion. Spread \u2248 1\u20132 years \u2014 mild arbitrage only, not a decisive gap. No meaningful horizon arbitrage exists.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n| Component | Score (0\u20133) | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Business quality | 2 | Narrow moat, strong underwriting culture, excellent financials |\n| Valuation attractiveness | 2 | 1.2\u00d7 book attractive vs 20%+ ROE, but requires normalization; no MoS |\n| Macro alignment | 1 | Rates tailwind vs growth headwind; neutral-to-negative |\n| Thesis clarity | 3 | Measurable KPIs: combined ratio, GWP growth, BVPS growth, buyback pace |\n| Execution traceability | 3 | 2.5 years of public track record, clean balance sheet |\n| **Conviction** | **11/15 \u2014 High** | Minus 0.5 (macro), minus 0.5 (data-pack basis discrepancy lowers confidence) \u2192 **effective 10/15** |\n\nDecay: 1 step per 6 months without confirming evidence.\nSizing: High conviction \u2192 6\u20138% guideline, but capped -1% (macro), -1% (narrowing moat), -1% (no MoS) \u2192 **4\u20135% weight**. Behavior (Section 5.5) adds no further cap.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u